    "problem_statement": "# Removed method `get_default_arg_name` breaks custom argument naming\n\nI discovered an issue with the `Parser` class in webargs. The method `get_default_arg_name` has been removed, but it was previously used to customize how argument names are derived when using `use_args` without an explicit `arg_name`.\n\n## Problem Description\n\nIn the current implementation, the default argument name is hardcoded to `f\"{location}_args\"` directly in the `use_args` method, rather than using the customizable `get_default_arg_name` method.\n\nThis breaks any code that subclasses `Parser` and overrides `get_default_arg_name` to customize the default argument naming scheme.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nHere's a simple example that demonstrates the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom webargs import fields\nfrom webargs.flaskparser import FlaskParser\n\nclass MyParser(FlaskParser):\n    def get_default_arg_name(self, location, schema):\n        # Custom naming scheme that no longer works\n        if location == 'json':\n            return 'body'\n        return super().get_default_arg_name(location, schema)\n\nparser = MyParser()\n\n@parser.use_args({'name': fields.Str()}, location='json')\ndef my_view(body):  # This expects 'body' as the parameter name\n    return f\"Hello, {body['name']}!\"\n```\n\nThe above code would previously work fine, but now it fails because the parameter is always named `json_args` instead of `body`.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nThe `get_default_arg_name` method should be preserved, and the `use_args` method should call it to determine the default argument name when none is explicitly provided.\n\nThis would maintain backward compatibility for users who have customized the argument naming scheme by overriding this method."
